[size=5][b]ForgePlanner[/b][/size]
<br />
<br />A crafting planner that lives inside Valheim. Press [b]F7[/b], build a shopping list, and see exactly how much ore, wood and hide you need — including the smelting, the coal, and which station you have to stand at.
<br />
<br />Everything is read from your running game: recipes, conversions, item names, icons, trader price lists and your language. Nothing is hardcoded, so the numbers keep matching after a game patch instead of going quietly stale.
<br />
<br />[line]
<br />
<br />[size=4][b]What it does[/b][/size]
<br />
<br />[list]
<br />[*][b]Plan several items at once.[/b] Three bronze picks and two maces, not one item at a time. Double-click a row to add it.[/*]
<br />[*][b]Quality levels 1-4.[/b] The upgrade surcharge is taken from the game, not guessed. Valheim doubles it per level (1, 2, 4), which is where most spreadsheets and wikis get it wrong.[/*]
<br />[*][b]Raw materials or recipe materials.[/b] Either break everything down to ore, wood and hide, or stop at what the workbench actually asks for.[/*]
<br />[*][b]Smelting included.[/b] Coal, smelting time and how many loads each station needs.[/*]
<br />[*][b]Counts what you already have.[/b] Your inventory plus nearby chests.[/*]
<br />[*][b]Tells you which stations you need[/b] - and whether one is standing nearby, how far away, in which direction, and at what level. If yours is too low, it says which level the plan wants. If there is none, one button adds the station itself to the plan.[/*]
<br />[*][b]Names the trader for anything you cannot find.[/b] Some materials drop nowhere; the line says who sells it and for how much.[/*]
<br />[*][b]Buildings too.[/b] Walls, floors, workstations - the hammer menu is in the same catalogue.[/*]
<br />[*][b]Filter by biome[/b], with the same colours as the companion website.[/*]
<br />[*][b]Search and eight tabs:[/b] All, plus Weapons, Armour, Tools, Food, Materials, Buildings and Other. Search works in both English and Russian whatever language the game is running in.[/*]
<br />[/list]The window borrows the game's own font, panel and button art at runtime, so it matches your UI scale and your language.
<br />
<br />[line]
<br />
<br />[size=4][b]Made for a group[/b][/size]
<br />
<br />Alone the sums are small. On a shared server they are the boring part of the evening: how much iron the base still owes for four sets of armour, how much coal that turns into, how many trips to the smelter that means, and whether anyone remembered to bring fine wood.
<br />
<br />Plan the whole order in one go - four maces, three shields, two sets of armour - stand next to the storage, and the window subtracts what is already there. It counts your own inventory and every chest within range, so a base's shared storage is read once for the whole plan instead of being tallied chest by chest on a second monitor.
<br />
<br />[size=4][b]A wiki you do not have to alt-tab to[/b][/size]
<br />
<br />It does not replace the Valheim wiki and does not try to: no lore, no drop tables, no strategy, no pictures of bosses.
<br />
<br />What it does carry is the part you actually look up in the middle of a session. What a thing costs. What the same thing costs at quality 2, 3 and 4. Which station it needs and at which level. What has to go through a smelter or a kiln first, and how long that takes. Which biome a material comes from. And, for the handful that drop nowhere at all, which trader sells it and for how many coins.
<br />
<br />A trimmed wiki, then - but the trimmed part is the part you were going to search for anyway, and this one is read from your own installation, so it cannot fall behind a game patch the way a wiki page can.
<br />
<br />[line]
<br />
<br />[size=4][b]Spoiler-free by default[/b][/size]
<br />
<br />A planner opened in your first hour would otherwise retell the whole game: a thousand entries with flametal weapons at the top. So a biome stays locked until the boss before it falls. Meadows, Black Forest and Ocean are always open - you get there on your own.
<br />
<br />Locked biomes dim rather than disappear, and the whole thing is one switch in Settings if you would rather see everything. If you have already beaten the bosses, you lose nothing: it is all open.
<br />
<br />The victory keys are read from the boss prefabs themselves, so the mod does not carry a list of boss names that a future update could quietly invalidate.
<br />
<br />[line]
<br />
<br />[size=4][b]Usage[/b][/size]
<br />
<br />Press [b]F7[/b] in a loaded world, or use the [b]ForgePlanner[/b] button on any crafting station's panel.
<br />
<br />The cursor is released while the window is open, and the game behaves exactly as it does when you press [b]E[/b] at a workbench: no swinging, building, walking or hotbar switching by accident. Press [b]F7[/b] or [b]Esc[/b] to close.
<br />
<br />[line]
<br />
<br />[size=4][b]Installation[/b][/size]
<br />
<br />[list=1]
<br />[*]Install [url=https://thunderstore.io/c/valheim/p/denikson/BepInExPack_Valheim/]BepInExPack Valheim[/url] 5.4.2350 or newer.[/*]
<br />[*]Unpack the archive and drop [b]Forgeplan.dll[/b] into [b]BepInEx/plugins/[/b].[/*]
<br />[/list]Client-side only. It does not touch saves, world data or networking, so it is safe to add to an existing world and safe to remove again - uninstalling leaves nothing behind but the config file.
<br />
<br />[line]
<br />
<br />[size=4][b]Configuration[/b][/size]
<br />
<br />[b]BepInEx/config/dev.forgeplanner.forgeplan.cfg[/b], written on first run.
<br />
<br />[code]
<br />[General]
<br />  Open           F7             Hotkey that opens the planner
<br />  Russian        false          Window labels in Russian
<br />  HideUnreached  true           Lock biomes until their boss is beaten
<br />
<br />[Stock]
<br />  CountChests    true           Count nearby chests, not just your backpack
<br />  Radius         20             How far a chest counts as yours, in metres
<br />
<br />[Station]
<br />  Button         true           Show the ForgePlanner button on station panels
<br />  Corner         BottomRight    Which side of the panel the shelf sits on
<br />  OffsetX        14             Shelf offset from the panel edge, in points
<br />  DropDown       0              How far below the panel edge to drop the shelf
<br />
<br />[Debug]
<br />  SelfTest       false          Dump the parsed catalogue to the log on world load[/code]The three [b]Station[/b] keys are there for repair rather than taste: the crafting panel belongs to the game, and a future Valheim update may move it. If the button ends up in the wrong place, it can be put back without waiting for a new release of the mod.
<br />
<br />[line]
<br />
<br />[size=4][b]Companion website[/b][/size]
<br />
<br />[url=https://forgeplanner.pages.dev]forgeplanner.pages.dev[/url] runs the same calculation in a browser, for planning before you launch the game.
<br />
<br />The two are kept honest about each other: the mod reads the live game, the site reads extracted game data, and a parity harness compares the catalogue, conversions and per-level costs entry by entry. Four real calculation bugs were found that way in a single day - potential idols counted as ingredients, bronze taken from scrap instead of ore, a five-ingot recipe beating the one-ingot one, and a wrong upgrade curve.
<br />
<br />Progression is the one thing the site cannot do: it does not know which bosses you have beaten.
<br />
<br />[line]
<br />
<br />[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]
<br />
<br />[list]
<br />[*]Valheim (tested against 1.0.15)[/*]
<br />[*][url=https://thunderstore.io/c/valheim/p/denikson/BepInExPack_Valheim/]BepInExPack Valheim[/url] 5.4.2350 or newer[/*]
<br />[/list][size=4][b]Source and licence[/b][/size]
